{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>For if you shall surely keep.</b> One must know why He repeated saying <b>shamor tishmerun</b>; furthermore, what watch is this? Perhaps it intends to warn about the observance of the commandments that HaShem commanded to make a safeguard for its keeping, and here He commanded Israel concerning that watch — that they should keep it. And that is His saying <b>shamor</b>, which is the watch that our Rabbis of blessed memory enacted, <b>tishmerun</b> meaning: you shall keep it." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend along this line: ״If you keep״ — meaning, if you make a safeguard for the commandments, thereby you will be assured that you will <b>keep</b> all the commandments; but without a safeguard you will stumble in some of the commandments. For example, the commandment of Shabbat: if they do not add from the profane onto the holy, there is reason to fear they will stumble in the very essence of Shabbat." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend to command concerning the commandments, that they be kept both from oneself and from others. And the intention in this is that a person should not say: it is enough for me if I guard myself lest I transgress one of the commandments, and I have no obligation to be troubled by the nullification of commandments on the part of others. Therefore the word of HaShem comes: <b>shamor</b> — you <b>tishmerun</b> the commandments, meaning, lest they be nullified by others. And with the pleasantness of these matters, He gave the reason that HaShem obligated Israel through mutual responsibility (areivut). For the totality of the obligation rests upon the commandments — that they not be nullified in the world by any Jew. And therefore whenever one is nullified, a breach has been made in the bride of our houses — for the Torah was given to us as an inheritance (morashah); do not read morashah but me'orasah (betrothed) (Pesachim 49b) — and upon a man rests the obligation of guarding his betrothed lest she be disgraced by others. Likewise this matter: the fear of HaShem, which they instilled in us from Heaven — rests upon us the obligation of guarding Her lest they lay hands on what the Torah does not desire. And this will be through the power that will be in the hands of the commanded ones to rule with a rod and prevent oppression and wickedness, and also to reprove with pleasant words of love and affection to turn the hearts of people to straighten the crookedness of the heart. And that is His saying <b>to do it, to love</b> etc., <b>to walk in all</b> etc., <b>and to cleave to Him</b>. Meaning, His saying <b>to do it</b> — in the manner of their saying (Yevamot 63a): ״comely in expounding and comely in fulfilling.״ And our Rabbis of blessed memory also said (Berakhot 6a): ״Any person in whom there is fear of Heaven — his words are heard,״ for one who comes to warn people and turn them to the straight path must first himself do all the commandments. And that is His saying <b>to do it</b>; and then he will instill the love of HaShem in the heart of the listener. And that is His saying <b>to love</b> — meaning, the verb goes out to the second party, that he activates the effect of his love, blessed be He, in the hearts of people lest they lay hands on the Torah of HaShem. And His saying <b>to walk in all His ways</b> — He included in the phrase ״all His ways״ to say: even things that are not perceived among the created beings. And that is along the lines of their saying of blessed memory (Bava Metzi'a 85a): ״If you bring forth what is precious out of what is vile, you shall be as My mouth״; and they further said of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 19b): ״as if he created him״; and accordingly — just as HaShem creates and forms, so too does one who instills His love, blessed be He, in the heart of the people of HaShem." "And His saying <b>and to cleave to Him.</b> We have already presented precious introductions, that all the souls of the people of Israel are from the supernal quarry, the portion of HaShem, for all of them are attached to HaShem, as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:4): ״וְאַתֶּם הַדְּבֵקִים בַה׳״. And through transgressions the soul distances from its attachment, and when a person returns before HaShem, it returns to become attached to Him. And that is His saying <b>and to cleave to Him</b> — meaning, to attach to Him the branches of holiness that need to be attached to Him." "<b>Conclusion of the parashah of Ekev</b>"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:51.702587047Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}